        {
          prompt: "Apply it: run the parentheses test on a real ZDub prospect's ad, then rewrite it to close the loopholes.",
          fields: ["Original ad with your skeptic's parentheses:", "Rewrite with each objection closed:", "The moment-of-need trigger you used (like the renewal notice):"],
          rubric: ["Opens with a frank statement of benefit", "Every objection is answered by the very next line", "Includes a real moment that tells the customer when to act", "Drops clichés like 'competitive pricing' and 'you'll be glad you did'"],
          model: `Original (Sacramento landscaper): “Family-owned since 1998. (So?) Quality landscaping at affordable prices. (Everyone says that.) Free estimates! (Then a hard sell.) Call today! (Later.)”

Rewrite: “Text us three photos of your yard and get a written price within 24 hours. (Do they have to come out?) No visit needed for the first estimate. (Will they upsell me?) The price covers the plan you picked, line by line, with nothing added on the day. (What if I don't like it?) You approve the drawing before anyone digs. (When should I do this?) Send the photos the next time you look at that dead lawn and sigh.” [Only use policies they really have.]

Trigger: the moment of looking at the dead lawn.`
        }
