      id: "woa-021",
      part: "p1",
      chapter: 21,
      chapterTitle: "The World’s Best Traffic Builder",
      pages: "52–53",
      title: "The World’s Best Traffic Builder: traffic without a message is worthless",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 1998)", section: "Chapter 21 · The World’s Best Traffic Builder · pp. 52–53", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "if you want your ad to be remembered when the prospect has need of your product, you’ve got to have something attached to your name.", page: "52" },
        { text: "it’s a good ad that just happens to generate immediate traffic.", page: "53" }
      ],
      idea: `A client says, "I'd like to do a traffic builder." Roy says, "One traffic builder, coming right up!" and describes his favorite. Hire three actors dressed as police officers. The first stands in the street and diverts all traffic into your parking lot. The second waves the cars into spaces. The third stands at the front door and blows a whistle to herd people into the store. When the place is packed, they rip off the uniforms and start selling.

**The joke has a point.** Roy admits his idea might be slightly dumber than yours, but only because it's illegal. The crowd it brings in would be no *less qualified* than the crowd most traffic-building schemes produce. He imagines the customer: "I came for the inflatable pink gorilla on your roof and the free hot dogs you mentioned on the radio. Now that I see you're a car dealer, I suddenly want a new car!" Gimmicks attract people who want the gimmick.

**The bigger target is "getting your name out."** Roy puts traffic builders in the same school of thought. Getting your name out might be enough to win an election, but for a business you need something *attached* to your name so people remember you when they need what you sell. (That connects directly to the mental trigger in Chapter 16.)

**When a traffic builder is fine:** if it reinforces your market position or communicates your unique selling proposition, it's more than a traffic builder. It's a good ad that happens to bring in traffic right away. Even then, that kind of traffic is never predictable; a promotion that packs the store one week may bring nobody the next.

**Roy's two options:**
1. Write meaningful ads that people remember when they need your product, or
2. Write ads to bring in immediate traffic.

If you choose option 2, he says he knows someone who can help: the hot dog company's guy, who works with a pink gorilla.

**The 2026 version:** "like, share and tag to win" giveaways, cheap link-click campaigns and engagement bait. They create activity, not memory. Judge any traffic tactic by one question: does it attach a reason to choose this business to the business's name? This is the twin of Chapter 20. The sale and the gimmick are both quick fixes; one cuts your price and the other wastes your attention.`,
